S8 — Canonical product cutover and physical removal
===============================================================================

Status: preparation started; no consumer is accepted and no stage is sealed.
Planning baseline: S7B 723aa500c2022d40a2ac3f0f6d35f0fb39772509 on S7A
b237d74a316a88fbf6ce48b6e417592479e4dc3e. Both are still qualifying.

1. Acceptance and ownership
-------------------------------------------------------------------------------

Keep three draft PRs: codex/syntax-s8a-consumers -> S7B;
codex/syntax-s8b-execution -> S8A; codex/syntax-s8c-cutover -> S8B.
Develop the actual C branch while A/B remain open. Approve C last.
A and B cannot be sealed until the planned C candidate has demonstrated the full
product matrix with retiring parser/lowering code physically removed. A's local
consumer-readiness gate is provisional while explicitly B-owned blockers remain.
B cannot seal with a failing or incomplete deleted-code C rehearsal.

Only C changes shipping source routing. A/B prepare canonical product interfaces
and executable witnesses. No production parser selector, fallback, shadow run,
or canonical-to-old-AST adapter is allowed. Preparation must not cache two trees
as competing source authorities. Keep the existing S1 census frozen at 27
contracts / 29 calls until the coordinated C change.

The readiness inventory extends source-parser-consumers.tsv by consumer-id; it
does not replace that authoritative caller census. There are exactly 10 A-owned
contracts / 11 calls and 17 B-owned contracts / 18 calls. Every contract has one
integration owner. The removal inventory links route:<consumer-id> to the same
owner and also tracks transitive interfaces, caches, serialization, features,
build products and test dispositions. Planned entry-point names are proposals,
not claims that a product witness already exists.

S8A owns CLI formatting/classification, retained source/cache/index interfaces,
file/memory resolution, and browser capture/source/presentation preparation.
S8B owns compilation/configuration, interactive execution and combined native,
server/bundle/browser products. Shared files do not change contract ownership.
C owns the reviewed routing, export/dependency removal and permanent enforcement.
An implementation gap discovered on C returns to A, B or its upstream owner.

2. Prerequisites remain with their owners
-------------------------------------------------------------------------------

The baseline continuation/closeout records still have open S4 semantic execution,
S6 activation, S7A declaration bindings and complete rendering, and S7B final
qualification gates. A clean code review is not their product acceptance.
S7A's local rendering work is not included in this baseline. Rebase qualified
upstream changes before the final rehearsal; do not copy an unfinished renderer
or declaration implementation into S8 to bypass those gates.

Wrong recognition/recovery/typed structure -> its canonical syntax owner.
Missing language execution, type/shape/control semantics -> S4 semantic owner.
Missing document declaration/scope/render/capture behavior -> S7A.
Streaming finality/continuations/deltas/performance -> S7B.
Old-tree storage, public consumer interfaces and canonical-result handling -> A/B.
Mechanical adoption and the approved deletion -> C.

3. First implementation increments
-------------------------------------------------------------------------------

A1: freeze linked ownership, removal/test inventory, API decisions and acceptance
matrix. Add retained SourceDocument ownership with exact source coordinates and
strict indexing admission; no production route switch. Prove that malformed
source remains reportable and cannot publish partial resolver facts.

A2: prepare file/memory/module/compiler cache integration using one canonical
snapshot. Fallible insertion must be atomic on error. Infallible builders retain
malformed source plus diagnostics. Usable resolved bindings require the S7A
handoff; index-only tests do not finish a resolver row.

A3: integrate qualified rendering/classification/capture at product-facing
interfaces. Exercise actual CLI and browser entry points on C, including errors,
Unicode, CRLF, hidden/disabled/named/Mika outputs and source replacements.

B1: start with compiler tree-accepting APIs, mixed compute and bundle/browser
loader boundaries. Preserve host capabilities, initializers, observable symbols,
module resolution, compute partitioning, memory planning and artifact ownership.
A CanonicalSourceFrontend unit test alone does not prove ProgramCompiler ready.

B2: integrate configuration and interactive lifecycle. Consume every StreamUpdate
before advance or deliberately coalesce with the original baseline. Only actual
submission finality authorizes one execution. Test replacement/reset/clear,
failed submission, cancellation/discard and editing/stream transitions.

B3: complete the actual deleted-code C candidate and execute every product route
and distribution gate. Fix discovered behavior in its named owner and restack C.

C: draft deletion/routing early. An incomplete first draft records exact compiler
and consumer blockers; it is not a successful rehearsal. Final C adds no new
semantics, renderer, parser recovery, runtime owner or artifact format. Freeze
named routing/API/removal changes before B acceptance, not a line-count budget.

4. Public API and packaging decisions
-------------------------------------------------------------------------------

Retained source: SourceDocument owns a canonical SyntaxSnapshot and its exact
source revision. Derived typed indexes are projections, not a second tree. Do
not persist process-local node IDs as durable cache identities. Replacing source
invalidates indexes and presentation/compilation projections for the prior
revision. No format-old-tree/reparse path survives.

Normalization: retain raw source in canonical storage. Frozen trimmed interfaces
must be reviewed individually; changing to raw is a proposed API change until
that row's positive/negative coordinate tests pass. Empty reset is intentional;
transport exhaustion is not final EOF. There is no universal trim operation.

Remove tree-accepting compiler methods only with canonical source/scope methods
that preserve each method's responsibility (inputs, initialization, static
symbols, interactive observability and mixed compute). Do not silently drop
methods by replacing all of them with a scalar compile wrapper.

ResolvedSource.syntax_tree and with_syntax_tree/replace_syntax_tree are planned
breaking removals. C replaces them with canonical source ownership. A/B do not
add another tree field to the shipping record. SourceIndex DTOs remain usable;
old-node traversal disappears. CanonicalDocumentIndex retains local Mika owners.

nodes::Program is the retiring AST. MechSourceCode::Program(Vec<MechSourceCode>)
is a source-asset grouping type, not that AST. Its public migration is an explicit
B decision after transitive inspection; a matching name is not deletion evidence.
Similarly preserve shared source-coordinate/runtime types if still required.

The retained syntax namespace also exposes an older Prototype document route
and a ParserImplementation selector outside the S1 Nom caller census. A owns
its removal/test inventory; C removes those public routes and prototype owners.
DocumentSession and DocumentStream already use canonical owners. The final
absence gate must cover prototype APIs as well as Nom and lower/legacy. Shared
scanner/recovery utilities stay with the canonical owner; do not delete by name.

Bundle payloads must use retained source and the accepted ProgramArtifact/
bytecode representation. B must prove producer -> actual loader -> execution.
Retired serialized Program payloads and stale caches require regeneration or
explicit rejection; no decoder/parser fallback. Keep current artifact ABI/version.
The exact envelope and cache discriminator are B-owned decisions still open;
C may not design them. Apply release/API notes with C. Rollback is a qualified
pre-cutover revision, never a runtime parser-selection flag.

5. Product and distribution acceptance matrix
-------------------------------------------------------------------------------

Reuse .github/workflows/ci-full.yml and existing distribution/browser machinery.
A/B run pre-cutover checks; C runs the real switched product. Add missing named
product witnesses to those jobs. No parallel CI system or special executable.

- Syntax: isolated full/base --tests, grammar conformance, reduced features,
  no_std native/WASM, all streaming/publication/resource/growth tests.
- CLI: standard and full distributions; format source/HTML, executable
  classification, run source and inline native mixed compute, positive results
  and positioned invalid-source diagnostics through actual commands.
- Runtime: isolated full_source/resident-routing-source; retained resolver
  bindings, program artifact/inputs/initializers, config, observable REPL and
  canonical document output/state. Repeat failed/discarded candidate behavior.
- Native: existing generated end-to-end and hosted CLI tests, accepted artifact
  admission and execution, frozen EKF and mixed-compute target canaries.
- Server/bundle: actual bundle generation, serve/load, source retention, output
  placeholders, accepted artifact execution; stale AST payload/cache rejection.
- Browser: existing formatted/served document browser smoke and standard/compute
  bootstraps; REPL source/highlighting/capture and interactive lifecycle through
  actual exported APIs; WASM bytecode admission and execution.
- Distribution: check-static-distribution-profiles.sh isolated source/runtime/
  compiler profiles, maintained reduced/no-source dependencies, packaging and
  generated products; static distribution and warning/architecture contracts.

Cargo workspace feature unification cannot stand in for an isolated profile.
A required test target with zero executed tests is incomplete evidence. Record
intentional feature exclusions separately and run their positive counterpart.

6. Evidence and removal closure
-------------------------------------------------------------------------------

Every readiness row requires named positive/negative product witnesses, C route,
removed dependencies, feature profiles and exact evidence SHA/result. Initial
status is planned, never accepted from a function's existence. Preparation tests
are identified separately from real routed C product evidence.

Every affected test is retained unchanged, retargeted to canonical APIs, or
removed only for an exclusively retired implementation detail. The initial
manifest conservatively marks scanned affected files for retargeting. Mixed
behavior/parity files must split maintained assertions before removal. The
textual seed scan is not a complete Rust dependency graph; C builds and the
existing semantic alias/cfg census expose its remaining closure.

For every C rehearsal record: B parent SHA, C commit and tree IDs, routing and
removal manifest identities, commands/profile/toolchain, CI run/head or test-merge
identity, executed test counts/results, and named A/B/upstream blockers. Do not
infer execution from a green badge. Publish candidate evidence on the PR to avoid
self-referential commit hashes. Keep old results labeled historical after a
representation/semantic change. Final C needs full agreed qualification.

Replace the frozen caller assertion with zero-retiring-call enforcement only in
C. Preserve adversarial alias/cfg scanner tests. Verify retired files and exports
are absent, not feature-disabled. Historical inventory cannot exempt live code.
Final review must find no unresolved ownership or public API decisions.

Progress is reported as consumer witnesses, closed removal dependencies, routed
products and exact qualification results. Starting counts: 0/27 consumers
accepted; 0/29 shipping routes changed; deletion closure unproven.
